,"H3045":"to know","H669":"Ephraim","H3808":"not","H3582":"to hide","H4480":"from","H6258":"now","H2181":"to fornicate","H2930":"to defile","H5414":"give","H4611":"deed","H7725":"return","H413":"to(wards)","H430":"God","H7307":"spirit","H2183":"fornication","H7130":"among","H853":"[Obj.]","H3068":"LORD","H6030":"to dwell","H1347":"pride","H6440":"before","H3782":"to stumble","H5771":"crime","H1571":"also","H3063":"Judah","H5973":"with","H6629":"flock","H1241":"cattle","H3212":"to walk","H1245":"to seek","H4672":"to find","H2502":"to rescue","H898":"to act treacherously","H1121":"child","H2114":"be a stranger","H3205":"to beget","H398":"to eat","H2320":"month","H854":"with","H2506":"portion","H8628":"to blow","H7782":"trumpet","H1390":"Gibeah","H2689":"trumpet","H7414":"Ramah","H7321":"to shout","H1007":"Beth-aven","H310":"after","H1144":"Benjamin","H8047":"destroyed","H3117":"day","H8433":"rebuke","H7626":"staff","H539":"be faithful","H8269":"ruler","H5253":"to remove","H1366":"boundary","H8210":"pour","H4325":"water","H5678":"fury","H6231":"to oppress","H7533":"to crush","H2974":"be willing","H1980":"went","H6673":"command","H6211":"moth","H7538":"rottenness","H7200":"see","H2483":"sickness","H4205":"wound","H804":"Assyria","H7971":"depart","H3377":"`great`","H1931":"he/she/it","H3201":"be able","H7495":"to heal","H1455":"to cure","H595":"I","H7826":"lion","H3715":"lion","H2963":"to tear","H5375":"raise","H369":"nothing","H5337":"to rescue","H4725":"place","H5704":"till","H834":"which","H816":"be guilty","H6862":"narrow","H7836":"to seek"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",1,null,"challoner","O priests.... What is said of priests in this prophecy is chiefly understood of the priests of the kingdom of Israel; who were not true priests of the race of Aaron; but served the calves at Bethel and Dan."],["challoner",7,null,"challoner","Children that are strangers.... That is, aliens from God: and therefore they are threatened with speedy destruction."],["challoner",10,null,"challoner","As they that take up the bound.... That is, they that remove the boundary, encroaching on the property of their neighbors: figuratively: going beyond the boundary of the laws of God."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Of priests. What is said of priests in this prophecy is chiefly understood of the priests of the kingdom of Israel; who were not true priests of the race of Aaron, but served the calves at Bethel and Dan. (Challoner) — They had the name of priests, and pretended to act as such, 3 Kings xii. (Worthington) — There were some apostates among them, chap. iv. 6. But they lost all authority. — To them. Literally, “to the watch:” speculationi. Hebew, “at Maspha,” (Haydock) in Galaad, where a profane altar was erected, chap. vi. 8. (Calmet)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Depth, or pits of fire, where victims were sometimes thrown. (Iphigen.) (Grotius) — By substituting th for t, we might read, “they have dug pits to take them,” chap. ix. 9., and Josue xxiii 13. (Calmet) — Idolatry leads to the abyss. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Known. Fornication had darkened their intellect. (Calmet)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Answer. Septuagint, “be humbled.” It appears openly, so as to deserve condemnation. (Haydock)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Them. He will receive their victims no longer, Isaias i. 11. (Calmet) — In vain do they expect to escape by this appearance of sanctity, while they continue in sin. (Haydock)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Strangers. That is, aliens form God: and therefore they are threatened with speedy destruction. (Challoner) — Their offspring is rebellious, and deserves no longer to be called my people, chap. i. 9. — Month. Every month the Assyrians shall come upon them; (Chaldean; St. Jerome) or, in the space of one month, they shall perish. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “the mildew shall eat them and their portions.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Back. Bethel lay northwest of Benjamin. The two tribes would hear the distress of Israel, that they might beware and avoid the like misconduct. (Calmet) — The captivity is here described. (Worthington)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","That. Literally, “faith,” (Haydock) that my word shall come to pass."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Bound. This was a capital crime, under Numa, and forbidden, Deuteronomy xix. 14. (Calmet) — Juda hoped to seize what was abandoned. (St. Jerome) — They deferred doing penance, and removed the boundaries set by their fathers, (Theodoret; Calmet) the virtuous patriarchs, whom they would not imitate."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Oppression. Literally, “calumny.” (Haydock) (Isaias lii. 4.) — The Assyrians had no just reason for attacking Israel, though their crimes called for punishment (Calmet) from God. (Haydock)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Avenging. Hebrew and Septuagint Jareb, (St. Jerome) which some explain of the king of Egypt; others understand the Assyrian; (chap. x. 6.) while most suppose that Ephraim applied to Phul, and Juda sent to a protecting king, Theglathphalassar, 4 Kings xvi. 7., and xvii. 4. (Calmet)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Lioness. Hebrew and Septuagint, “panther.” (Haydock) — The Assyrians, instead of assisting, proved the ruin of both kingdoms."],["haydock",15,null,"jerome","Place; to heaven. I will abandon my temple. (Calmet) — Face: “they will seek the absent.” (St. Jerome)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"]}}